      "text": "To determine how far Jana will walk in 10 minutes, we need to use her rate of walking. We know that it takes her 24 minutes to walk one mile. Therefore, her walking rate is:\n\n\\[ \\text{Rate} = \\frac{\\text{Distance}}{\\text{Time}} = \\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}} \\]\n\nWe need to find out how far she will walk in 10 minutes. Let's denote the distance she walks in 10 minutes as \\( d \\) miles. Using the walking rate, we can set up the following equation:\n\n\\[ \\frac{d \\text{ miles}}{10 \\text{ minutes}} = \\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}} \\]\n\nTo find \\( d \\), we can solve for \\( d \\) by cross-multiplying:\n\n\\[ d \\times 24 = 1 \\times 10 \\]\n\n\\[ d \\times 24 = 10 \\]\n\n\\[ d = \\frac{10}{24} \\]\n\nNow, we need to simplify the fraction \\(\\frac{10}{24}\\):\n\n\\[ d = \\frac{10 \\div 2}{24 \\div 2} = \\frac{5}{12} \\]\n\nTo express \\(\\frac{5}{12}\\) as a decimal to the nearest tenth, we perform the division:\n\n\\[ \\frac{5}{12} \\approx 0.4167 \\]\n\nRounding 0.4167 to the nearest tenth gives us 0.4.\n\nTherefore, the distance Jana will walk in 10 minutes is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{0.4} \\]",
