      "text": "First, let's determine Jana's walking rate in miles per minute. We know that it takes her 24 minutes to walk one mile. Therefore, her rate is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Rate} = \\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find out how far she will walk in 10 minutes at this rate. The distance traveled is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\text{Rate} \\times \\text{Time}\n\\]\n\nSubstitute the rate and the given time into the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\left( \\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}} \\right) \\times 10 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expression:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\frac{10}{24} \\text{ miles}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify \\(\\frac{10}{24}\\), we find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 10 and 24, which is 2. Divide both the numerator and the denominator by 2:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{10 \\div 2}{24 \\div 2} = \\frac{5}{12}\n\\]\n\nTo express \\(\\frac{5}{12}\\) as a decimal, perform the division:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{5}{12} \\approx 0.4167\n\\]\n\nRounding this to the nearest tenth:\n\n\\[\n0.4167 \\approx 0.4\n\\]\n\nThus, Jana will walk approximately \\boxed{0.4} miles in 10 minutes.",
