      "text": "To determine how far Jana will walk in 10 minutes, we start by finding her walking speed in miles per minute. We know that Jana takes 24 minutes to walk one mile. Therefore, her speed is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Speed} = \\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}} = \\frac{1}{24} \\text{ miles per minute}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to calculate how far Jana will walk in 10 minutes. We do this by multiplying her speed by the time she will be walking:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\text{Speed} \\times \\text{Time} = \\frac{1}{24} \\text{ miles per minute} \\times 10 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nNow, we perform the multiplication:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\frac{10}{24} \\text{ miles}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify \\(\\frac{10}{24}\\), we find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 10 and 24, which is 2. We divide both the numerator and the denominator by 2:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{10 \\div 2}{24 \\div 2} = \\frac{5}{12}\n\\]\n\nTo express \\(\\frac{5}{12}\\) as a decimal, we perform the division:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{5}{12} \\approx 0.416667\n\\]\n\nRounding 0.416667 to the nearest tenth, we get:\n\n\\[\n0.416667 \\approx 0.4\n\\]\n\nThus, the distance Jana will walk in 10 minutes is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0.4}\n\\]",
